<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chichester</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chichester is a cathedral city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It began as the Roman town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noviomagus-reginorum">Noviomagus Reginorum</a>, capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/regni">Regni</a>, where an inscription names the client king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togidubnus">Togidubnus</a>. The bishopric moved there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selsey">Selsey</a> in 1075, and its cathedral dates largely from the 12th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chichester.jpg" alt="Chichester" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tilman2007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Market_Cross_Chichester_1026826_20230815_0001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chichester stands on the River Lavant, a winterbourne, south of its gap through the South Downs. Many ancient routeways converge there, which made it a good site for settlement. The oldest part lies within the medieval walls, which are built on Roman foundations, and the plan of the city, with its North, South, East and West streets radiating from a central market cross, is inherited from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. It is the county town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sussex">West Sussex</a> and its only city.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>There is no recorded evidence of a settlement of any size before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The area is believed to have played a significant part in the Roman invasion of 43, and military storage structures have been found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fishbourne-roman-palace">Fishbourne Roman Palace</a>. The city centre stands on the foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noviomagus-reginorum">Noviomagus Reginorum</a>, capital of the Civitas Reginorum. Stane Street ran from its east gate to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and an amphitheatre was built outside the walls around 80.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aelle-of-sussex">Ælle of Sussex</a> and his three sons annexed the area late in the fifth century and that the city was renamed after his son Cissa. Historians regard this as a myth, since no evidence shows reoccupation before the 9th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> rebuilt Chichester as a burh, probably in 878 or 879, and it was one of the larger burhs, rated at 1500 hides in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghal-hidage">Burghal Hidage</a>.</p><p>After the Norman Conquest the cathedral founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selsey">Selsey</a> in 681 was moved to Chichester after the Norman Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> of 1075 decreed that sees should be centred in cities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> of 1086 records 300 dwellings and a population of 1,500. Roger de Mongomerie, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a>, received the town and built Chichester Castle soon after 1066. The city was besieged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1642 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-english-civil-war">First English Civil War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Chichester Cathedral, the Cathedral Church of the Holy Trinity, is the seat of the Anglican Bishop of Chichester and has a separate bell tower, unusual in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It holds a shrine to Saint Richard of Chichester, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-bernstein">Leonard Bernstein</a>'s Chichester Psalms were commissioned for it. Chichester Cross, built in 1501 as a covered marketplace, stands where the four main streets meet. The Novium museum, opened in 2012, preserves the Roman baths.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chichester” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Blake wrote much of Milton while living in a cottage at Felpham in Sussex; local tradition at Lavant, near <b>Chichester</b>, holds instead that he wrote this poem in a public house there.</blockquote><a href="/songs/and-did-those-feet-in-ancient-time">Jerusalem (And did those feet in ancient time)</a></li><li><blockquote>An inscription from <b>Chichester</b> dedicating a temple to Neptune and Minerva names Tiberius Claudius Togidubnus as great king in Britain, showing that he held Roman citizenship and royal status.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-regni">Kingdom of the Regni</a></li></ul></section>
